\begin_layout Abstract
This report aims to document and verify the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Flaps 2D
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 simulation tool, developed by myself since February 2012, to investigate
 the physics of laser-matter interaction in nanostructured materials.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Context of the simulation tool
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The development of this work has been supported during 11 months by the
 french National Research Agency (ANR) under the project 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Ultrasonde
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 leaded by Tatiana Itina, Laboratoire Hubert Curien, Saint-Etienne, France.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Today, I'm still working on the development of this code which, at term,
 allows to study the absorption by a structured material irradiated by a
 femtosecond laser.
 The code has been designed so that material can be also polarized by a
 high voltage.
 The coupling of polarized materials with laser illumination allow tomographic
 reconstruction of atomic lattices in 3D with metals, dielectrics and oxides.
 The code can also be used to study the transport of free-carriers in nanostruct
ures considering the effect of the geometry.
 This aspect is also a promiseful domain for a better understanding of the
 carrier and heat transport properties at the nanoscale.
 
\end_layout

