\begin_layout Paragraph
Numerical integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several possibilities can be tried to deal with the non-linearity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use a higher-order precision scheme on the time integration for the 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{s}$
\end_inset

 equation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use a Runge-Kutta solver.
 However, there gonna be diffusive terms enabled later, which will not be
 mathematically clean with such a scheme.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Iterate the solution and ensure convergence 
\emph on
before going to the next time-step 
\emph default
(can really multiply the calculation time)
\emph on
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying a higher-order precision scheme allows to obtain solutions closer
 to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exact
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 solution.
 Here, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exact
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 solution is considered to be the one given by the 4th order Runge Kutta
 scheme (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ode45
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 function of Maple and Matlab).
 However, using an eulerian scheme does not provide a sufficiently good
 convergence of the solution even at the 4th order.
 The choice of the fitting function using the Van Driel's form interestingly
 allows to obtain a solution equal to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exact
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 one.
 The origin of this numerical recipe is not understood yet.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It should be mentioned that in the all the cases considered in this section,
 the energy conservation has been verified and did not show any deficiency.
 The serious non-linearity that we face in this section is important for
 the topic and can strongly affect the final results given by the code.
 
\end_layout

