\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Control-of-the"

\end_inset

Control of the energy conservation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the mesh is not regular and that finding analytical solution to the
 equation using an arbitrary contour in a reasonable duration is beyond
 the capabilities of the author, it has been decided to study the energy
 conservation as a function of time.
 The timescale of the processes investigated by this code is near from the
 nanosecond reaction of the system, which is 6 orders of magnitude larger
 than the laser pulse duration (of about 40 fs to 500 fs).
 The validity of the time-integrator is thus of major importance in this
 multi-scale problem, the connexion to the spatial steps is thus straightforward.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Energy of the carriers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since we model an external laser pulse irradiating the sample by its intensity
 evolution with time and space through the function 
\begin_inset Formula $I(t,x,y)$
\end_inset

, then we will express the total energy considering the invariance by translatio
n with the 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 axis, normal to the orientation of this document plane.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The total energy study will be expressed in 
\begin_inset Formula $J/m$
\end_inset

.
 Actually, the energy of the carriers in 2D is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dE_{e}\left[J.m^{-3}\right]=dE_{e,kin}+dE_{pot}=d\left(C_{e}T_{e}\right)+d\left(E_{g}n_{e}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}$
\end_inset

 [m
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

] is the control volume of each 2D cell of the mesh, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{e,kin}$
\end_inset

 expresses the thermal energy of conduction band electrons, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{e,pot}$
\end_inset

 expresses the potential energy of the conduction band electrons.
 This latter expression can be also written for holes.
 However, since the origin of the potential energies is taken at the edge
 of the valence band, then holes have a potential energy equal to 0.
 Thus we can write 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dE_{h}\left[J.m^{-3}\right]=dE_{h,kin}+dE_{h,pot}=d\left(C_{h}T_{h}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Integrating over the two dimensions of the problem leads to write the total
 energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{e,h}$
\end_inset

 at a given instant in the whole sample volume by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{alignat*}{1}
\xi_{e}\,[J.m^{-1}] & =\iint dE_{e}\,d\mbox{\mathcal{V}}\\
 & =\iint\left(T_{e}dC_{e}+C_{e}dT_{e}+dE_{g}n_{e}+E_{g}dn_{e}\right)\,d\mathcal{V}
\end{alignat*}

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{alignat*}{1}
\xi_{h}\,[J.m^{-1}] & =\iint dE_{h}\,d\mbox{\mathcal{V}}\\
 & =\iint\left(T_{h}dC_{h}+C_{h}dT_{h}\right)\,d\mathcal{V}
\end{alignat*}

\end_inset

Band gap is a function of lattice temperature 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{s}$
\end_inset

 and carrier density 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}$
\end_inset

 expressed via 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Driel1987"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{g}(T_{s},n_{e})=1.17-4.73\times10^{-4}\frac{T_{s}^{2}}{T_{s}+636}-1.5\times10^{-10}n_{e}^{1/3}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Control of the laser energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we have chosen the unity to express the energy in the problem, we express
 now the total laser energy is by the expression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{sample}[J.m^{-1}](y)=\iint I(t,x,y)\,dt\,dx
\]

\end_inset

integrated over the diameter of the laser spot.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the shape of the surface is not flat, and the surface reflectivity
 is taken into account by the analytical solutions of Mie scattering, assuming
 a scattering on a 3D cylinder from which a plane section is studied: the
 intersection between the study plane and the entire 3D space.
 Consequently, the calculation of the overall reflectivity and transmittance
 by the problem shape is rather difficult.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calculation of the tip absorptance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We try to solve this problem by studying the energy absorbed by the nanometric
 sized sample via 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{absorbed}[J.m^{-1}]=\iiint\alpha I(t,x,y)\,dt\,dx\,dy
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the conditions of the experiments (
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=40$
\end_inset

 fs, 
\begin_inset Formula $F=10$
\end_inset

 J/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=343$
\end_inset

 nm), the order of magnitude of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{absorbed}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-4}$
\end_inset

 J.
 In order to calculate the overall absorbed laser energy, we define 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\frac{\xi_{absorbed}}{\xi_{laser}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{laser}=F\times\mathcal{V}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

