\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:Optical-energy"

\end_inset

Optical energy as a function of the mesh precision for an homogeneous energy
 distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla I(x,y)=0$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $F=10$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $J.m^{-2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=40$
\end_inset

 fs.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Optical-energy"

\end_inset

 presents the total optical energy deposited in the sample as a function
 of the mesh properties in case of an homogeneous distribution of laser
 energy.
 This allows to calculate the minimal mesh and time-step parameters required
 to reach convergence in the numerical sample volume and the convergence
 of the intensity calculation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calculation of the incident laser energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The intensity at the tip apex interface is defined by the following formula
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{laser}\left[J\right]=\iint\overrightarrow{S}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\,d\mathcal{A}\,dt
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{S}$
\end_inset

 is the Poynting vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{n}$
\end_inset

 is the normal to the 3D surface of the cone, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 is the area element defined around the normal vector.
 The intensity is defined by the flux of the Poynting vector by the surface
 of area 
\begin_inset Formula $d\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 In the plane of the simulation 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(xOy\right)$
\end_inset

, we can write the following relation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\overrightarrow{S}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}=I\left[x,y_{\partial\Omega}\right]\cos\theta_{\partial\Omega}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tan\theta_{\partial\Omega}=\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}.
\]

\end_inset

Since we consider only two dimensions with an invariance by translation
 along the coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, we can build the quantity
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{laser}\left[J.m^{-1}\right]=\iint\overrightarrow{S}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\,d\mathcal{L}\,dt
\]

\end_inset

using the contour of the cone in the plane of the model 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(xOy\right)$
\end_inset

, where the projection of the Poynting vector at the interface is known.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The arc length along the curve 
\begin_inset Formula $x(y)$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}=\int\left|\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right|\,dy
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{laser}\left[J.m^{-1}\right]=\iint I\left[x_{\partial\Omega}\left(y\right),y,t\right]\cos\left(\arctan\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right)\left|\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right|\,dy\,dt.
\]

\end_inset

For a constant intensity defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $I(x,y,t)=I_{0}e^{-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{t}{\sigma_{t}}\right)^{2}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{t}=\frac{\tau}{2\sqrt{2\ln2}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{0}=\frac{F}{\tau}\sqrt{\frac{4\ln2}{\pi}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F=10$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $J.m^{-2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=40$
\end_inset

 fs, it is found 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Verification of the calculated intensity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{laser}=\iint\left(\sigma_{1}+\alpha_{fcr,e}+\alpha_{fcr,h}\right)I_{0}(t,x,y)\,d\Omega\,dt
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\xi_{opt}=\iint\left(\sigma_{1}+\alpha_{fcr,e}+\alpha_{fcr,h}\right)I(t,x,y)\,d\Omega\,dt
\]

\end_inset

We define the numerical trust value which corresponds to the ratio between
 the energy absorbed contained the solid, with the energy given by the laser
 intensity distribution.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\eta=\frac{\xi_{solid}}{\xi_{opt}}
\]

\end_inset

We also define the absorptivity by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\overline{A}=\frac{\xi_{opt}}{\xi_{laser}}
\]

\end_inset

 which is the effective absorptivity of the laser pulse considering the
 shape of the cone.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EnergyBalanceMie"

\end_inset

 shows the results for 343 nm using different meshes.
 It is clear that :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

numerical trust index
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 demonstrates that the size of the mesh is of major importance for obtaining
 a good energy conservation between the field and the solid.
 But why, since the equations are using the same mesh ? It can be due to
 the fact that the strongest absorption zone at 343 nm is at the boundary,
 and that the first energy contained in the cells of the boundary is neglected.
 Thus, at 515 nm, we shall need less cells to reach the energy balance between
 the fields and the solid.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the effect of the meshgrid is negligible in the energy balance.
 It will take its importance only for the energy diffusion conservation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A problem is identified in the definition of the optical absorption which
 simply cannot be so small.
 Indeed, at 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=343$
\end_inset

 nm, the penetration depth of the field is nearly 9 nm, which is clearly
 smaller than the size of the cone.
 Thus, it is not possible that the absorbed energy is so small.
 Its definition might be wrong or reveals a numerical problem.
 
\end_layout

