\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:EnergyBalanceMie"

\end_inset

Optical energy as a function of the mesh precision for an inhomogeneous
 energy distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla I(x,y)\neq0$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $F=10$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $J.m^{-2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=40$
\end_inset

 fs.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Trust
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta=\frac{\xi_{solid}}{\xi_{opt}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left\lceil \right\rceil $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Generation and control of the mesh
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Definition of the contour
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Regularization of the mesh
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mesh is built by defining a contour and solving 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\left(\begin{array}{c}
x\\
y
\end{array}\right)=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

 by a simple iterative method
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=\frac{1}{4}\left(x_{i-1,j-1}+x_{i-1,j+1}+x_{i+1,j-1}+x_{i+1,j+1}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\frac{1}{4}\left(y_{i-1,j-1}+y_{i-1,j+1}+y_{i+1,j-1}+y_{i+1,j+1}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Round about 70 000 iterations are required to reach convergence of the mesh.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This method is optimum on a single core since convergence rate of this technique
 is rather good (how to quantify ?) in respect of the global calculation
 time.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Optimization of the code
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Optimization is a big word for telling many things.
 First, we have designed a model which is actually already simplified to
 decrease the amount of necessary operations.
 In this section, purpose is to perform optimizations over the code, especially
 over the way to perform the operations on a single processor, and to decrease
 the waste of operations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sequential code: checking the bottlenecks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the Intel Loop Observer software (or GNU profiler), we can monitor
 the number of calls to the loops and number of calls to the functions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By performing a first test during one night, I already figured out that
 a function was using 25% of the calculation time (sequential) despite that
 it was not necessary.
 The number of operations was found to be 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{12}$
\end_inset

 after one night.
 Actually, this operation was required once, and then replaced by calls
 to memory.
 But is this call to memory really saves time instead of performing such
 simple operation each time ? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the next section, we focus on optimizing the way to perform calculations
 with several cores, to parallelize the operations.
 First, we will define the tools used to quantify the efficiency of a parallel
 code, and we will also perform some verification allowing to avoid the
 unnecessary operations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Calculation of the code velocity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CPU=\frac{N_{timesteps}}{t_{current}-t_{initial}}\times N_{threads}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{current}$
\end_inset

 is the CPU time captured just before performing the outputs, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{initial}$
\end_inset

 is the CPU time captured at the initialization of the code.
 Hence, CPU is supposed to be constant within the execution time of the
 code.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $CPU$
\end_inset

 is built to be independent to number of cores used.
 In this purpose, it becomes possible to calculate the speedup 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 in core-number-independent way.
 Of course, the speedup may increase while increasing the number of cores,
 hopefully, until saturation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename home/thibault/Documents/Codes/Flaps/Optimization/20150208-SpeedUp.eps
	width 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
CPU efficiency as a function of core number.
 Note that these calculations were performed with a homogeneous energy source
 in the sample.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One understands that the definition of CPU is dependent to the frequency
 of the CPU clock.
 Hence, it is not reasonable to compare the CPU quantity between different
 computers.
 However, we can normalize the CPU by the velocity of the code in sequential
 mode on a single computer in order to build the speed up 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

, a quantity that it is intrinsic to the efficiency of the code.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\eta(N)=\frac{CPU_{N-cores}}{CPU_{1-core}}
\]

\end_inset

Hence, it looks that speedup of our parallelization method is greater than
 1, but is lower than 2 with the available number of cores.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Changing the order of the loops
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main loop of the code is consuming 30% of the calculation time in sequential
 mode.
 By changing the order of the loops, and by parallelizing the inner or outer
 ones, we can obtain serious improvement over the speedup.
 Fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Optimization1"

\end_inset

 demonstrates a very strong influence over the order of the loop iterations
 and distribution.
 Despite that one solution looks to be more efficient than the others, it
 is not sure if such optimization conserves the physical meaning of the
 result.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename home/thibault/Documents/Codes/Flaps/Optimization/20150208-OptimizeLoopOrder.eps
	width 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
